s x increases.","The mean of the x-values equals the largest probability.","Every possible x-value has the same probability."],"e":"A discrete probability distribution must assign a probability in [0,1] to every possible value and the probabilities must sum to 1. Here 0.50+0.30+0.15+0.05=1.","stimulusKey":"prob2","statsSetType":"probability"},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.9","skill":"3.D","q":"For the device-failure model, what is E(X), the expected number of failures per day?","correct":"0.75","distractors":["0.50","1.00","1.50"],"e":"E(X)=ΣxP(X=x)=0(0.50)+1(0.30)+2(0.15)+3(0.05)=0.75. This is a long-run average and need not be a possible integer outcome on a single day.","stimulusKey":"prob2","statsSetType":"probability"},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.10","skill":"4.B","q":"Could the device-failure distribution shown be Binomial(n, p) for some n = 3 and constant p?","correct":"No; the probability ratios do not match a binomial model with three independent equal-p trials.","distractors":["Yes; support on 0, 1, 2, and 3 is sufficient for Binomial n = 3.","Yes; probabilities summing to 1 is sufficient to establish independent equal-p trials.","No; a binomial random variable is restricted to the values 0 and 1."],"e":"Having support 0–3 is not sufficient for a binomial model. Binomial probabilities must arise from three independent binary trials with constant success probability; the displayed probabilities do not have that required form.","stimulusKey":"prob2","statsSetType":"probability"},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.8","skill":"3.A","q":"A game pays X = $0, $5, or $20 with probabilities 0.70, 0.25, and 0.05, respectively. Which quantity is a random variable?","correct":"The dollar amount X paid on one play","distractors":["The fixed probability 0.70","The list of all three probabilities","The statement that the game has three outcomes"],"e":"A random variable assigns a numerical value to the outcome of a random process. The payment X varies from play to play according to the stated probability distribution."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.9","skill":"4.D","q":"A carnival game's winnings have mean $1.20 and standard deviation $4.50. Which interpretation of the mean is correct?","correct":"The long-run average winnings per play approach $1.20.","distractors":["A player will win exactly $1.20 on most individual plays.","About 68% of plays will be within $1.20 of the mean.","The probability of winning is 1.20."],"e":"The expected value is the long-run average outcome of the random variable. It does not have to be a possible result on any single play and does not itself specify a probability."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.10","skill":"3.D","q":"For X ~ Binomial(n = 80, p = 0.25), what are the mean and standard deviation of X?","correct":"μ = 20 and σ = √15 ≈ 3.87","distractors":["μ = 20 and σ = √20 ≈ 4.47","μ = 60 and σ = √15 ≈ 3.87","μ = 0.25 and σ = √0.1875 ≈ 0.433"],"e":"For a binomial random variable, μ=np=80(0.25)=20 and σ=√[np(1−p)]=√[80(0.25)(0.75)]=√15≈3.87."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.11","skill":"3.C","q":"Adult heights are approximately normal with mean 170 cm and standard deviation 6 cm. Approximately what proportion lies between 158 cm and 182 cm?","correct":"About 95%","distractors":["About 68%, the one-standard-deviation empirical-rule value","About 99.7%, the three-standard-deviation empirical-rule value","About 50%, because the interval is centered at the mean"],"e":"The interval 158 to 182 is μ ± 2σ because 170 ± 12 = 170 ± 2(6). By the empirical rule, about 95% of a normal distribution lies within two standard deviations."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.12","skill":"4.C","q":"The population distribution is strongly right-skewed with finite mean μ and standard deviation σ. What happens to the sampling distribution of x̄ as the sample size increases substantially?","correct":"Center μ, decreasing SD σ/√n, and increasingly normal shape","distractors":["Its mean increases toward the population maximum and its standard deviation remains σ.","Its mean remains μ but its standard deviation increases as σ√n.","Its shape becomes more right-skewed because averaging preserves every population feature."],"e":"For independent sampling, x̄ is centered at μ with standard deviation σ/√n. The central limit theorem says its distribution becomes approximately normal for sufficiently large n even when the population is skewed."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.3","skill":"3.C","q":"In the sampling-mean simulation, what does the SD 4.